ref_fine: references for SPDYN's analytic nonbonded pair term
==============================================================

The ref files hold double-precision energies computed with GENESIS's
lookup table at the default density (table_density = 20).  Under

  [ENERGY]
  nonbond_precision = MIXED

the native GPU core evaluates Ewald real space and Lennard-Jones in closed
form (ewald_evaluation = ANALYTIC, the default where the table has that
form), so it has no table interpolation error.  It therefore departs from
ref by the interpolation error of the density-20 table, which on some tests
exceeds tolerance_single (for example 1.2e-4 in dppc VDWAALS).

ref_fine is the same test run by the double-precision CPU code with a
finer table:

  [ENERGY]
  table_density = 2000

At that density the interpolation error is about 1e-4 of the default's, so
ref_fine carries the model, not the table.  Nothing else in the input
changes.

For a run whose output shows nonbond_precision = MIXED and not
ewald_evaluation = TABLE, in a test that has a ref_fine file, test.py
grades each term against the reference that computes it the same way:

  VDWAALS           against ref_fine.  Closed-form Lennard-Jones is the
                    fine-table limit; its table rows are zero at the cutoff.
  every other term  against ref.  Electrostatics is closed-form inside the
                    cutoff and follows the run's own table over its last
                    interval beyond the cutoff (see below), which ref
                    contains and ref_fine does not.

Every other run, and every test without ref_fine, is graded against ref as
before.  The tolerances are unchanged (MIXED runs report precision = mixed
and use tolerance_single).

The 1-4 terms read the default-density table in every mode, so a MIXED
run keeps their interpolation error (up to 1.2e-5 in VDWAALS on these
tests); it is within tolerance_single.

ref_fine files exist only for the tests whose MIXED run departs from ref
by more than tolerance_single: the dppc PME tests (PME_*, VVER_*, VRES_*),
jac_param27/PME_FSW, nano_material/VVER_NPT_BUSSI1 and pops/PME.  Every
other test is graded against ref.

The last interval.  The linear table carries the pair term from its value
at the cutoff to zero over its last interval (cutoff to
cutoff*sqrt(density/(density-1)), 12 to 12.31 A at the default density),
and the analytic term evaluates that interval the same way, so that the
energy has no step at the cutoff.  A density-2000 table has almost no such
interval, so ref_fine lacks that contribution.  It is negligible where
pme_alpha is 0.34.  nano_material/VVER_NPT_BUSSI1 sets pme_alpha from
pme_alpha_tol (0.2266, erfc(alpha*rc) = 1.2e-4); there ref and ref_fine
differ by 34.2 kcal/mol in ELECT and by 0.90 in VDWAALS, the
double-precision native run equals ref in every digit, and the MIXED run is
within 0.23 kcal/mol (1.7e-5) of ref_fine in VDWAALS and within 1.9
(1.8e-6) of ref in ELECT.  Against ref alone its VDWAALS is off by 7.7e-5,
against ref_fine alone its ELECT by 3.4e-5.
